  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: As a frontal boundary continues to slowly sink\nsouthward into the southern Mid-Atlantic this weekend, a ridge\nof high pressure will become our dominant weather feature. This\nwill mean partly-mostly sunny days and mainly clear nights\nthrough Sunday. Given our recent wet stretch of weather and\nabundant soil moisture in many areas, late night and early\nmorning fog will be common, particularly in the river valleys of\nnorthwest and north-central PA.\n\nTemperatures/humidity levels will be seasonable and comfortable\nthrough Saturday. On Sunday, as low-level winds begin to switch\ninto a more southerly direction, afternoon temperatures and\nhumidity levels will start to creep upward. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: During next week, there is good agreement amongst\nour various ensemble prediction systems that an expansive\nsummer-time ridge axis at upper levels of the atmosphere will\nflex its muscle and expand northeastward from the southern \nstates and middle of the CONUS. \n\nThere are two primary consequences for the sensible weather\nacross the Commonwealth as a result of the above mentioned\nscenario. First, very warm and increasingly humid weather will\nsettle in, even as we flip the calendar to September. Second, as\na northwest flow aloft develops over much of the Mid-Atlantic\nand Northeast with embedded disturbances, and overruns the \npreviously mentioned warm, sticky air, episodic showers and\nthunderstorms are expected. Although fine details are not yet\napparent, this overall pattern often favors locally strong-\nsevere thunderstorms and heavy downpours. As we draw closer to\nnext week, we hope to add more clarity with respect to the \ntiming and coverage of showers and storms.",
